	Dean’s Number & Secondary Vortex Flow in Folded Bends
When a required port length  exceeds the internal cabinet depth, designers fold the port using a 90-degree L-elbow or 180-degree U-bend. This introduces two distinct physical regimes: a linear acoustic wave effect (active at all playback levels) and a nonlinear high-SPL aeroacoustic flow effect (governed by Reynolds and Dean numbers).
At high playback levels, air oscillating in the port reaches peak velocities  where viscous forces can no longer damp nonlinear inertial vortex forces (), triggering turbulence, acoustic compression, and broadband "chuffing" noise with spectral components extending up to 10 kHz. As established by Vanderkooy, Backman, and Roozen et al., straight-edged ports suffer severe asymmetric exit-jet vortex shedding and compression when port air velocities exceed roughly 10 m/s, whereas aerodynamically flared ports remain linear up to higher Reynolds numbers (, with a practical upper limit in loudspeaker ports on the order of ).
When high-velocity port air negotiates a curved bend of inner cross-sectional radius  and centerline bend radius , centrifugal forces act perpendicular to the main streamwise axis, characterized by the dimensionless Dean number ():



As the Dean number increases in a 90-degree elbow, centrifugal pressure gradients drive high-momentum core fluid toward the outer wall while low-momentum boundary-layer fluid sweeps inward, rolling up into counter-rotating secondary Dean vortices perpendicular to the primary streamwise flow. Simultaneously, the adverse pressure gradient along the inner radius causes boundary-layer separation, reducing the effective aerodynamic cross-sectional area and triggering turbulence at lower playback levels than in a straight pipe.
Crucially, even at small-signal linear levels, a curved bend does not have an acoustic length equal to its geometric centerline arc length . Because the inner wall presents a shorter path than the outer wall, the acoustic wave traveling along the inner path arrives earlier while being partially pulled back by neighboring waves traveling longer outer paths. Time-of-flight () and impedance correlations demonstrate that both the effective acoustic length correction factor () and the characteristic impedance correction factor (, where ) follow power-law functions of the curvature ratio , independent of the absolute tube radius:






