3. Spatially Distributed Porous Absorption (Delany-Bazley-Miki Models)
Delany-Bazley, Miki, Garai-Pompoli, and JCA Formulations
When acoustic waves propagate through fibrous or open-cell porous media—such as fiberglass, Rockwool, Dacron Hollofil II polyester, Basotect melamine foam, or 3D-printed open-cell TPMS Gyroid lattices—viscous drag between the oscillating air and the pore walls converts kinetic energy into heat, while thermal conduction between the air and the solid skeleton shifts the thermodynamic compression cycle from adiabatic at high frequencies toward isothermal at low frequencies. As L. J. Bradbury and Martin J. King established for transmission-line enclosures, at low frequencies viscous drag also couples the mass of flexible fibers to the air, significantly reducing the effective phase speed of sound .
